# csplit — real-world examples

## Split a file at each line matching a pattern

```sh
csplit input.file '/PATTERN/'
```

## Control output file names with prefix and suffix

```sh
# prefix-0.extension, prefix-1.extension, ... for every match
csplit -f 'prefix-' -b '%d.extension' input.file '/PATTERN/' '{*}'
```

## Homelab: split a combined SQL dump into one file per table

```sh
csplit -f table- -b '%02d.sql' full-dump.sql '/^-- Table structure/' '{*}'
```

## Homelab: break journalctl output at each boot marker

```sh
journalctl --no-pager | csplit -f boot- - '/-- Boot/' '{*}'
```

## Split a config file at every section header, no zero-length files

```sh
csplit -z httpd.conf '/^<VirtualHost/' '{*}'
```

| Flag | What it does                          |
| ---- | ------------------------------------- |
| -f   | output file prefix (default xx)       |
| -b   | numeric suffix format (printf style)  |
| -z   | suppress empty output files           |
| -s   | quiet — don't print file sizes        |
| {*}  | repeat the pattern as often as possible |
